  "Question": "Given that \\(x=1\\), \\(y=-2\\) is a solution of the system \\(\\begin{cases}ax+by=1\\\\bx-ay=7\\end{cases}\\), find the value of \\(a+b\\).",
  "Choices": {},
  "Correct Answer": "4",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "Substitution gives \\(a-2b=1\\) and \\(b+2a=7\\). From the first equation, \\(a=1+2b\\). Substitute into the second equation: \\(b+2+4b=7\\), so \\(b=1\\) and \\(a=3\\). Therefore, \\(a+b=4\\).\n\nAlternative method 1: Eliminate b by addition\n\nBecause \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=-2\\) satisfy both equations, substitution gives\n\\[\na-2b=1,\\qquad 2a+b=7.\n\\]\nMultiplying the second equation by \\(2\\) makes its \\(b\\)-term cancel the first equation's \\(b\\)-term when the equations are added:\n\\[\n(a-2b)+2(2a+b)=1+2\\cdot7,\n\\qquad 5a=15.\n\\]\nThus \\(a=3\\). The first equation then becomes \\(3-2b=1\\), so \\(b=1\\). Therefore, \\(a+b=4\\).",
